#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 780.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153061} :activity/id #uuid "610c3ee9-b5f3-57d8-b82e-cac1f007574a" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048694 :topic/id #uuid "b52b86c6-5073-5d60-a17c-32a308489156" :topic/math-academy-id 2638} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153061 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153355 :tutorial/content "Suppose that we want to evaluate the line integral with respect to arc length\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_Cf(x,y)\\:\\textrm{d}s\n$$\n\nfor some function $f(x,y),$ where $C$ is a curve in the $xy$-plane defined using plane polar coordinates as\n\n$$\nr = r(\\theta),\\qquad \\theta_1 \\leq \\theta \\leq \\theta_2.\n$$\n\nTo calculate this line integral, we need to recall the following facts:\n\n- A polar curve is just a special type of parametric curve in the parameter $\\theta,$ where\n\n$$\nx = r(\\theta)\\cos\\theta, \\quad y = r(\\theta)\\sin\\theta, \\qquad \\theta_1 \\leq \\theta \\leq \\theta_2.\n$$\n\n- The arc length element $\\textrm d s$ in plane polar coordinates is given by\n\n$$\n\\textrm ds = \\sqrt{r^2 + \\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm d r}{\\textrm d \\theta}\\right)^2}\\,\\textrm d \\theta\\,.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above formulas into our line integral, we get the following result:\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_C f(x,y) \\, \\textrm{d}s  = \\int_{\\theta_1}^{\\theta_2}  f(r\\cos\\theta,r\\sin\\theta) \\,\\sqrt{r^2 + \\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm d r}{\\textrm d \\theta}\\right)^2}\\,\\textrm d \\theta\\,.\n$$\n\n \n\nLet's go through an example in which we apply this result." :tutorial/id #uuid "f970309b-6c25-53f1-a3b5-b094ebe87109" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3987 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "1d46b30d-17ce-5fe6-a7be-dcb1352a6151" :step/math-academy-id 23719 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153062}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153062 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152160 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151949 :question/id #uuid "287a493f